ed to collaborate on this  fronts we have not received a response  yet but I would be cautious as to make  sweeping judgments about what the  situation there overall is but are you  preparing for the potential of increased  provocations well they have provoked  there has been a series of short-range  launches and you know these are  certainly unhelpful and we have urged  them to stop this is at a time when the  whole world is are trying to come to  grips with this  this very unprecedented health crisis  but we watch very closely and we are  prepared and I think based upon our very  robust defense posture and and readiness  based upon our strong us-rok security  alliance let's talk a little bit more  about that Alliance and your readiness  TIFF to face any potential threat from  the north you've mentioned that they've  already provoked you have actually been  trimming back on some joint military  exercises in an effort to engage the Kim  regime in dialogue the Carnegie  Endowment for international peace I said  the following about that pairing back  cancelling crucial exercises to tracks  from Seoul's very ability to manage the  threatening neighbor it has been  engaging with by downgrading South  Korea's military readiness and  compromising its ability to conduct  operations with US forces aren't you  concerned that you might not be ready if  Kim lashes out I have full confidence in  our military authorities in  collaboration with the US military  authorities to keep up that robust  preparedness despite the re construction  of the exercises you know the that is  done to support the diplomatic tracks  certainly but all the while making sure  that the preparedness is not in any way  undermined but it doesn't help  certainly that your relations with the  United States are not as good as they  once were you're currently at odds over  the defense cost-sharing the US has  rejected your offer of a 13 percent  increase in spending and instead is  demanding a five-fold increase how do  you want to come to an agreement and  what happens if you don't difficult I  admit but you know this is an alliance  that's developed over 67 years there  have been difficult issues in the past  that we've been able to work through you  know align a la allies have differences  and and I think the special measures  agreement this time is particularly  difficult but we are both the term  to find an agreement that we both can  agree to obviously it will take more  time and more effort but we are you know  I'm very confident that we will reach an  agreement  eventually Donald Trump has said the  following it's a question of whether  South Korea will contribute toward the  defense of their own nation we are  defending nations that are very wealthy  South Korea is a very wealthy nation  does he have a point we are increasingly  wealthy which is why our share of the  defense the stationing of the troops  have have increased over the years  initially the initial agreement is that  we will provide the land and the  facilities and the u.s. will provide for  the expenses of the troops stationing  now as Korea developed and became more  wealthy we have agreed to a special  measures agreement which is which is the  the title of this this Agreement and  increasingly more more of that share and  with that share that increase has to be  incremental it can't be 30 percent 50  percent over a one-year period there is  a methodology system there is a  budgetary system that our government has  to follow and in the end this has to go  through the National Assembly for  approvals so there are all these  parameters that we as government needs  to meet while negotiating with the  United States and I think our partners  across the table from the United States  are fully aware of this and as you're  sorting those through those parameters  we have the u.s. president not missing  one opportunity to say what a great  relationship he has with the North  Korean leader Kim jong-un and what a  nice guy he is Trump has said the  following we fell in love no really he  wrote me beautiful letters are you  concerned that Trump is speaking in such  high terms about the dictator to the  north and not about the democratically  elected president of South Korea I mean  your longtime allies well how do you  keep saying concern we're not concerned  I think the the relations between our  two governments at all levels  between the presidents between myself  and the Secretary of State is very  strong very tight and I think in  particular during this time of the COBIT  19 crisis the collaboration has been  exemplary so I'm not concerned I think  that's president Trump's unique way of  messaging and it also indicates his  clear desire to continue to engage at  that level the engagement with North  Korea this time around has been driven  very much by that top-level political  will both on the site of the United  States and also to a certain extent on  this part of our side as well so and  it's very important to keep that high  level political will  going for the continued engagement what  other options you have if if that you  know if that alliance breaks down I mean  have you become too reliant on the US  nuclear shield that they provide I am  not at all concerned about I don't think  that's up that's a scenario that we  anticipate I think we the Alliance is  very strong the u.s. is fully committed  to this the security of the the current  the South Korea and and the extension of  their their nuclear shield so I don't  know where you get that the possibility  of that happening I don't see that  possibility let's talk about China  because in the meantime China has been  using the coronavirus crisis as an  opportunity to assert itself in the  region there there's been an arrest the  arrest rather of democracy activists in  Hong Kong military pressure being put on  Taiwan and its neighbors in the South  China Sea even going so far as to sink a  Vietnamese fishing vessel it looks like  China is using the corona virus pandemic  as a regional power grab while other  major powers are busy at home surely  that must raise some red flags for you  well China and we have art we had our  difficult time in 2016-2017 post my  president's visit in December 2017 I  think we are largely recovering the  close economic and people-to-people ties  as far as the corona 19 virus is  concerned  it first happened in China and we were  one of the first to be hit because we  have lots of people traveling from back  and forth to China and I think the  collaboration on the COBIT 19 has been  has been very good we press for quick  and prompt sharing of information we  underscore the importance of that and we  demonstrate that with our own actions  and and we urge that on the part of  China as well we have economies under  pressure right now amid this pandemic  citizens health at stake what do you  think your part of the world will look  like after this pandemic ends  I think post corona virus the world will  certainly be different from pre kovat 19  I think all economies have been severely  hit by this you know we are rolling out  domestic economic stimulus packages for  for our strategic industries for small  and medium businesses for furloughed  workers for for households but that can  only go to a certain extent we are very  dependent on the our trade our  investment ties with overseas so we very  much hope that that also recovers and we  can't do that by ourselves so I hope do  you hope that our partners will also see  the self-interest in an you know for you  know recovering the global value chains  that the the trade and investments the  business trips and and you know I think  we there's two trends I think yes there  are tendencies of countries to become  insular and and and shutting of the  doors and and and parochial but I think  there is also an understanding that and  the virus has made it so clear that  these challenges are cross-border it  doesn't stop at the border and you may  close the border but the virus still  gets in and so there is a increasing  call for global coordination at various  fora at the UN at the whu-oh at the g20  and we do hope that it  beyond the talk that there will be  greater multilateral collaboration in  overcoming not just the the health  crisis but also the socio-economic  crisis as well South Korean foreign  minister kang kyung-hwa thank you for  joining us on complex oh thank you  [Music]  you
 

